WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:07.430
With Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 on the
horizon, it feels like the perfect time to

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:12.280
remind the world of exactly why the Guardians
are so great. These intergalactic superheroes

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.640
have battled the forces of evil for almost
fifty years now… or at least some incarnation

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.930
of the group has, anyway. If that sounds confusing,
then keep watching, because we've got plenty

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:24.080
of Guardians trivia you've probably never
heard before.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.100
The original Guardians

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.130
Sure, Star-Lord and Groot are awesome, but
they’re total newbies to the Guardians of

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:34.750
the Galaxy. The original team was created
in 1969 as a parallel to DC's Legion of Super

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.129
Heroes. The team was based in the 31st century,
and included very different members: Vance

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:44.589
Astro from Earth, Martinex from Pluto, Charlie-27
from Jupiter; and finally one you may almost

00:00:44.589 --> 00:00:50.050
recognize...Yondu from Centauri IV. The foursome
initially joined forces in Marvel Super-Heroes

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.030
#18 to battle an alien race known as the Badoon.
The team would soon grow to take on two additional

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:57.089
members: Starhawk and Nikki.

00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:59.460
A wild Star-Lord appears

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.979
Once upon a time, Marvel published a black-and-white
magazine called Marvel Preview. The experimental

00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:08.050
title provided creators with the opportunity
to do stories about characters a bit too far

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:13.190
outside the mainstream. Star-Lord was one
of them, first appearing in 1976. Although

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.830
the character continued to pop up here and
there over the years, it wasn't until the

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.860
2004 series Thanos that he officially returned.
In this incarnation, his backstory was tweaked

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.200
and his earlier adventures were ruled as being
outside Marvel's main continuity. But make

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.020
no mistake: the purists still remember.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.130
The Milano

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:34.050
You may have picked up that Star-Lord’s
cinematic ship is called The Milano, and no,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:38.990
it’s not named after the delicious cookie.
Director James Gunn tweeted in July of 2014

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.830
that the ship is actually named after actress
Alyssa Milano, which falls perfectly in line

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:47.530
with young Peter Quill’s 1988 abduction.
After all, the pubescent Quill disappeared

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:51.510
from Earth just as Milano was the most famous
16-year old on the planet due to her role

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.070
in Who’s the Boss?

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.150
I am Groot

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:59.360
It was all the way back in 1960’s Tales
to Astonish #13 that Groot made his debut.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.400
At the time, the creature of walking wood
came from Planet X, was much more talkative,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.460
and was bent on capturing humanity. Within
a few pages, Groot is defeated by a guy with

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.670
a box of termites, and it takes decades for
the merciless mahogany to turn up as anything

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.620
other than a guest-starring ginkgo. Groot's
reinvention took place around the same time

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:20.940
as Star-Lord's, thanks to Marvel's epic 2004
crossover storyline Annihilation: Conquest.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.480
And even though movie Groot was reduced to
a three-word script, consisting of…

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.280
"I am Groot."

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.900
"Yeah, you said that."

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.230
...voice actor Vin Diesel read his line over
a thousand times.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.930
"I am Groot…"

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:38.069
"Well, that's just as fascinating as the first
89 times you've told me that. What is wrong

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:39.630
with giving tree?"

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.580
During a red carpet interview with Marvel,
Diesel explained.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:48.340
"When I came into the recording booth, there
was a 50 page document. And on the left-hand

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:54.850
side, it said 'I am Groot.' And on the right-hand
side it would have a paragraph or a sentence

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:58.530
explaining what he was really meaning or what
he was really trying to say."

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.470
Drax the Real Estate Agent

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:04.400
Guardians of the Galaxy is pretty short on
origin stories, so trying to explain the history

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.830
of Drax the Destroyer is a little complicated.
In the comics, he begins his existence in

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:13.690
1973 as real estate agent Arthur Douglas,
who is then killed by Thanos, and later resurrected

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:18.440
by Thanos' father… for the sole purpose
of killing Thanos. After missing his chance

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:22.650
to kill his killer twice, Drax decides that
he may as well be a good guy. If you can’t

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:26.370
beat ‘em, and you can’t get sweet revenge
on your murderer... Join ‘em.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:26.780
Bug out

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.450
Bug has been a part of Marvel since 1979,
when the company obtained the rights to produce

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:34.550
a Micronauts comic, based on the toy line
of the same name.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:40.220
"Acroyear the enemy and the Micronaut space
warriors, all sold separately."

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.200
Bug was originally known as Galactic Warrior,
but because the comic character looked nothing

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.290
like the toy, Marvel changed his name and
made him their own. Bug bounced around the

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.870
Marvel Universe for years, eventually turning
up with the newer Guardians. James Gunn was

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.580
psyched about the idea of using Bug, but it
didn't work out. He broke the bad news on

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.050
the podcast The Movie Crypt:

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.340
"There was a really good chance Bug was going
to show up in the first movie."

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.180
"Oh really?"

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.040
"But he is not...we do not own him."

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.040
Also just barely missing from Guardians due
to rights squabbles? Fan favorite Rom the

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.970
Space Knight, a toy/comic crossover that Gunn
also wanted to include, but Marvel just didn’t

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:15.640
have the rights anymore.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.530
The Collector's collection

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:21.150
There are a lot of fascinating things to be
seen within the Collector's collection, many

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:25.350
of which are Easter eggs aimed at hardcore
Marvel fans. These include Cosmo the Space

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:30.199
Dog, what may or may not be Adam Warlock's
cocoon, and even Howard the Duck. In a nod

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:34.050
to his own film catalog, Gunn also included
some of the alien slugs that terrorize the

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:39.520
town in his 2006 film Slither. So, wait, does
that mean that film is part of the Marvel

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.570
Cinematic Universe? The mind boggles.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.630
Also originally intended to be part of the
collection: Stan Lee himself. Gunn didn't

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:51.110
think the first idea for his cameo was quite
up to par, however. That's a shame, too, because

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.000
the plan had been to show him as part of the
Collector's collection… and have him flip

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.580
off the camera.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.090
Road to Knowhere

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.120
You may have noticed that The Collector kinda
lives in a giant, floating head. The film

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:06.340
doesn’t go into it much, but that head formerly
rested on the shoulders of a Celestial. According

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.210
to Marvel’s official documentation,

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:13.680
“Celestials are a star-faring race of humanoid
aliens who possess untold cosmic power. Standing

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.070
two thousand feet tall, the Celestials are
clad in full body armor. No Earth being has

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:21.630
ever seen what they look like beneath their
armor or knows their origin."

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.990
Something else that remains unknown is how
the head of one of these things even fell

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:29.160
off, and ended up as a port of call at the
end of the universe. We don’t want to meet

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:32.110
the guy who can decapitate a half-mile tall
god.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.220
New Endings

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.949
Gunn's original vision for the end of Guardians
involved Peter Quill's grandfather, played

00:05:36.949 --> 00:05:40.669
by Gregg Henry. In an interview tied to the
Blu-ray release of the film, he explained

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:43.830
that Henry's moment in particular was just
too much of a downer to keep.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:47.810
"He has this photograph of Meredith (Peter
Quill's mother) and Peter as a little boy,

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.360
and he looks up at the stars, and we go up
to the stars, and it was really sweet.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.620
It means that he must have seen Quill getting
abducted at the end of that day and is still

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.490
waiting for him to return. But it was freaking
sad, so we took it out."

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.040
Star-Lord and Starman

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.639
Rock god David Bowie spent much of his career
focusing on space. He famously played an alien

00:06:03.639 --> 00:06:07.460
in The Man Who Fell to Earth, and even once
led a band called the Spiders from Mars.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:12.370
"You're really a freak. I don't mean that
unkindly—I like freaks."

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:17.070
So, he would've been a perfect fit for the
Guardians' galaxy. Unfortunately, Bowie didn’t

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:20.750
live long enough to appear in the sequel.
Like most of us, Gunn found out about Bowie’s

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:23.840
passing on Twitter, and took to Facebook to
express his grief.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.190
"Just a very short while ago Kevin Feige and
I were talking about a cameo role in Guardians

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:32.050
Vol. 2, and he brought up Bowie's name. I
told him nothing in the world would make me

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.930
happier, but I heard from common friends he
wasn't doing well. We heard back that he was

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.820
okay and it could potentially happen. Who
knows what that was about? But, for whatever

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.300
reason, it made my Twitter revelation more
of a surprise."

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.370
Oh, what might have been...

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:48.300
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.870
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know any weird Guardians of the Galaxy

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:53.180
facts you may know.

